! The tetrahedral member of this test, and the only tetrahedral incompressible
! flow case in the suite -- which is why it exists. Every other case here and in
! the elmerice set is quadrilaterals, hexahedra or prisms, so the tetrahedron
! constant in IncompressibleNSVec was inherited from ElasticSolve and never
! measured for this solver until this case was written.
!
! What it measures: the tetrahedron is INSENSITIVE to the coefficient, far more
! so than any other family. On this mesh the range 0.25 to 8 -- a factor of 32 --
! moves the norm by 0.4 %, where the quadrilateral moved 3 % over a factor of 12.
!
! Refining with "Mesh Levels" shows there is no well defined optimum to find. The
! error against MINI, in per cent:
!
!            c=1.0     c=1.5     c=2.0     c=3.0
!   L=1      -0.037    ~0        +0.037      --
!   L=2      -0.014    -0.011    -0.009    -0.008
!   L=3      -0.008    -0.016    -0.023    -0.031
!
! The crossing sits near 1.5 on the coarse mesh, above 3 on the next and below 1
! on the finest -- it wanders, because every one of these numbers is smaller than
! the discretisation error itself and the curve has no real minimum to lock onto.
! What IS clean is the convergence: at coefficient one the gap to MINI shrinks
! 0.037 -> 0.014 -> 0.008 % under refinement, so the stabilised pair converges to
! the MINI answer here as it does on the other families.
!
! Hence the inherited 0.0029 is left exactly as it is. Fitting a constant to a
! wandering crossing inside the noise would be worse than not fitting one.
!
! The mesh is ElasticStabilized's tet.msh, a unit cube of 386 tetrahedra. Only
! two of its surfaces carry a boundary id, 5 and 6, so the remaining faces are
! natural -- traction free -- which is what sets the pressure level here.
!
! An iterative solve is used deliberately: it reproduces a umfpack solve to every
! digit printed here, so the case needs no HAVE_UMFPACK guard and stays available
! in every build.

Header
  Mesh DB "." "tetmesh"
  Include Path ""
  Results Directory ""
End

Simulation
  Max Output Level = 5
  Coordinate System = Cartesian 3D
  Simulation Type = Steady
  Steady State Max Iterations = 1
  Output Intervals = 0
End

Constants
End

Body 1
  Name = "Body1"
  Equation = 1
  Material = 1
End

Equation 1
  Name = "Equation1"
  Active Solvers(1) = 1
End

Solver 1
  Equation = "NS-Vec"
  Procedure = "IncompressibleNSVec" "IncompressibleNSSolver"
  Stokes Flow = Logical True

! The point of the case. No "Element" with a bubble: _Init0 hands back "n:1"
! when this keyword is on, and it is written out so a reader need not know that.
  Pressure Stabilization = Logical True
  Element = "n:1"

  Solver Timing = Logical True
  Bulk Assembly Timing = Logical True
  Linear System Timing = Logical True

  Linear System Solver = Iterative
  Linear System Iterative Method = BiCGStabL
  Linear System Max Iterations = 1000
  Linear System Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-10
  Linear System Preconditioning = ILU1
  Linear System Residual Output = 0

  Steady State Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-5
  Nonlinear System Max Iterations = 1
  Nonlinear System Convergence Tolerance = 1.0e-6
End

Material 1
  Name = "Material1"
  Density = 1
  Viscosity = 1.0
End

Boundary Condition 1
  Name = "driven"
  Target Boundaries(1) = 5
  Velocity 1 = Variable Coordinate 2
     Real MATC "tx*(1-tx)"
  Velocity 2 = 0
  Velocity 3 = 0
End

Boundary Condition 2
  Name = "wall"
  Target Boundaries(1) = 6
  Velocity 1 = 0
  Velocity 2 = 0
  Velocity 3 = 0
End

! MINI ("p:1 -tetra b:1") on this mesh gives 5.66312290E-02, so the stabilised
! answer below is 0.037 % under it. The element saving is the largest anywhere in
! this work: MINI integrates a tetrahedron carrying a degree four bubble over 150
! points, the equal-order element over 4, and assembly falls 0.0142 s to 0.0022 s.
Solver 1 :: Reference Norm = 5.66103340E-02
